
Resource Map Widget — Build Specification

Status: Phase 1 design, ready to build
Author context: One Father's Love resource directory
Last updated: September 15, 2026

---

1. Purpose

The Resource Map Widget is a repeatable component attached to individual resource listings such as clinics, shelters, food banks, and other community resources.

It provides:

- Where the resource is on a map
- A rough visual representation of the distance between the person and the resource
- Basic contextual information such as time, weather, and calendar information

Phase 1 represents the relationship between one person and one resource. It does not provide real street routing.

The widget is designed to appear dozens to hundreds of times on a single page and to be reused across approximately 50 sites. Every architectural and interaction decision must therefore account for that scale.

Phase 1 implementation experience

Several AI systems were used during development and evaluation.

Claude.ai and Gemini were each unable to successfully produce the complete map-and-dashboard result after several attempts.

Claude did, however, contribute accurate specification conditions, particularly around interaction behavior, architectural constraints, and requirements that should not be violated.

ChatGPT was ultimately able to produce the working map-and-dashboard result that established the current Phase 1 design.

This distinction is important:

«A system's ability to describe correct conditions and a system's ability to successfully implement those conditions are not the same capability.»

The resulting specification therefore preserves the accurate conditions established during the design process while documenting the implementation experience separately.

---

2. Phase 1 Design

2.1 Three controls, three jobs

The widget deliberately separates its controls by function.

Control| Job
Map| Shows geography. Native pan and zoom remain available.
Journey slider| Moves the person's location indicator along the single person-to-resource path.
Information panel| Scrolls through resource information such as hours, eligibility, payment, and other decision information.

Earlier prototypes conflated these functions. In particular, a dashboard scrollbar was used to secretly move the map position. That approach was rejected because the control's appearance did not match its function.

Each control must have one understandable job.

---

2.2 Journey slider

The journey slider is:

- Horizontal
- Positioned below the map
- Full width of the map/card
- A 1D scrubber
- Restricted to the single path between the person's location and the resource
- Operable by both mouse and touch

The slider is not a freeform map control and does not move a map pin independently.

Behavior

At:

- 0% — the indicator represents the person's location
- 100% — the indicator represents the resource location
- Intermediate values — the indicator is interpolated between those two coordinates

Pointer Events should provide the common mouse/touch interaction path.

Rationale

There are only two meaningful endpoints in an individual resource card:

Person → Resource

A 1D control is therefore sufficient.

Direct manipulation of the marker on the map was rejected because:

1. A finger or cursor can obscure the marker being manipulated.
2. It conflicts with Leaflet's native map-pan gesture.
3. It introduces unnecessary 2D interaction when only one dimension of movement is meaningful.

---

3. Map Behavior

3.1 Native map interaction

The map must remain a normal interactive Leaflet map.

Users must be able to:

- Pan
- Zoom
- Use the visible zoom controls
- Inspect the resource's geographic position

The journey slider must not disable or interfere with native map interaction.

3.2 Resource coordinates

Every resource using the widget must provide, at minimum:

- Resource name
- Latitude
- Longitude

Optional resource configuration may include:

- Address
- Hours
- Service information
- Other information required by the resource card

The widget should not geocode every resource card. Resource coordinates should normally be provided as part of the resource's data.

---

4. Location Honesty

Location handling is a non-negotiable requirement.

The widget must never imply that a person's location is known when it is not.

4.1 Location states

The widget recognizes these states:

"location-pending"

The system has not yet established whether a usable person location is available.

"gps-location"

The person has granted permission and a usable GPS location is available.

Display label:

Your current location

"manual-location"

The person has supplied an address manually and that address has been successfully resolved to coordinates.

Display label:

Your entered location

"location-unavailable"

No usable person location is available.

Display label:

Location unavailable

In this state:

- The resource remains visible on the map.
- The resource remains usable.
- No person-location marker is shown.
- No distance-from-person claim is shown.
- No travel-time claim is shown.
- The system must not substitute a fake location.

Absolute rule

Never use a shared, default, downtown, city-center, or otherwise fabricated starting location to represent the person.

A missing location is preferable to a misleading location.

---

5. Distance and Travel-Time Honesty

5.1 Phase 1 calculation

Phase 1 uses the geographic coordinates of two points and calculates straight-line distance.

It does not calculate an actual street route.

Therefore the interface must describe the result as an estimate.

Example:

«Estimated distance: 2.4 miles
Based on straight-line distance»

The system must not present straight-line distance as though it were driving, walking, or transit distance.

5.2 Travel time

Phase 1 does not claim a real travel time unless a legitimate travel-time calculation has been implemented.

No false precision is permitted.

For example:

Not acceptable:

«2.4 mi — 5 min 37 sec»

That presentation implies a routing calculation that did not occur.

A real directions/routing service must be connected before the widget presents route-based travel time.

Hard rule

No seconds-level travel-time precision until an actual routing/directions API is providing the calculation.

---

6. Shared Page Architecture

The widget must be designed for repeated use.

A page may contain hundreds of resource cards. Expensive operations therefore cannot be performed independently by every card.

6.1 Shared location state

Geolocation should be requested once per page load.

The result should be stored in shared in-memory state and made available to every widget instance.

Individual cards must not independently trigger their own GPS request.

6.2 Shared manual location state

A manually entered location should likewise become shared page state after successful resolution.

The goal is:

One person's location → available to every resource widget on the page.

6.3 Shared weather state

Weather should be fetched once for the relevant location and shared across widget instances.

The implementation should also prevent duplicate simultaneous requests while the first weather request is still in progress.

---

7. Lazy Map Architecture

Leaflet maps are relatively expensive compared with ordinary HTML information.

A page containing hundreds of cards must therefore avoid creating hundreds of active map instances immediately.

7.1 Intersection Observer

A resource map should not initialize until its card approaches the user's viewport.

Intersection Observer should be used to detect when the map needs to become active.

Before initialization, the card may display a lightweight map placeholder.

7.2 Map lifecycle

The production implementation should establish a lifecycle strategy for maps that move far outside the viewport.

Possible approaches include:

- Suspending unnecessary map activity
- Removing distant Leaflet instances and recreating them when needed
- Another bounded-memory strategy

The final approach remains an implementation decision.

The requirement is that a page containing hundreds of resources must not accumulate unnecessary active map resources indefinitely.

---

8. Shared Widget Module

The widget should exist as a single shared JavaScript/CSS implementation.

For example:

- "resource-map-widget.js"
- Shared widget stylesheet

The approximately 50 participating sites should use the same underlying component rather than maintaining separate copied versions.

8.1 Per-resource configuration

Individual cards provide data such as:

- Resource name
- Latitude
- Longitude
- Address
- Hours
- Other resource-specific information

The component's behavior should remain centralized.

Principle

Resource data varies. Widget logic does not.

A bug fix or architectural improvement should be made once and then applied across participating sites.

---

9. Accessibility

The map is an enhancement. It must never be the only way a person can understand or use the resource information.

The widget must support:

- Keyboard operation of the journey slider
- Visible keyboard focus
- An accessible slider label
- Accessible map labeling
- Explicit button names
- Text-based presentation of important map-derived information
- Usable resource information when JavaScript, map tiles, GPS, or an external API fails

The journey slider must remain understandable without relying solely on visual movement of a marker.

The resource's address and other essential decision information must remain available through normal text content.

---

10. Map as an Enhancement

The map is not the resource itself.

If:

- Leaflet fails
- Map tiles fail
- GPS is unavailable
- Weather is unavailable
- An external API fails
- JavaScript functionality is partially unavailable

the underlying resource information must remain usable whenever possible.

The resource card's:

- Identity
- Services
- Eligibility information
- Access information
- Contact information
- Address
- Other decision information

must not depend on the map functioning.

Principle

«The map enhances resource discovery. It does not control whether resource discovery works.»

---

11. Cost and Request Budget

The widget is specifically designed for large-scale repetition.

Feature| Relative Cost| Phase 1 Rule
Calendar| Minimal| Local calculation per card
Distance calculation| Minimal| Local calculation
GPS| Expensive/user-sensitive| Once per page, shared
Manual geocoding| External request| Only when the person requests it
Weather| External/rate-limited| Once per relevant page/location, shared
Leaflet map| Memory/tile overhead| Lazy initialization per card
Real routing| External/API cost| Deferred

The architecture must prevent accidental multiplication of expensive operations by the number of resource cards.

---

12. Current Phase 1 Visual Structure

The current successful layout is intentionally simple:

Map

↓

Journey slider

↓

Compact information/dashboard area

The map occupies the upper portion of the widget.

The dashboard is below the map rather than beside it.

The map may be expanded through normal user interaction when additional map space is desired.

The dashboard remains compact because the widget may appear repeatedly on a page.

The exact visual treatment of individual dashboard elements remains subject to later tuning.

---

13. Contextual Information

The dashboard may provide lightweight contextual information including:

- Current time
- Weather
- Calendar information

These elements are supporting context, not substitutes for resource decision information.

The calendar is intentionally compact.

Further dashboard refinement should occur after the resource-card relationship is established rather than expanding the dashboard unnecessarily.

---

14. What Phase 1 Does Not Claim

Phase 1 is not a navigation system.

It does not yet provide:

- Actual street routing
- Turn-by-turn directions
- Accurate walking distance
- Accurate driving distance
- Transit routing
- Traffic-aware travel time
- Multi-stop routing

The visual path between the person and resource represents a geographic relationship, not an actual road route.

---

15. Deferred Phase 2 / Phase 1.5

15.1 Real routing

A future routing layer may provide:

- Street-based routes
- Walking routes
- Driving routes
- Transit routes where supported
- Real travel time
- More accurate distance

Possible services include Google, Mapbox, or OSRM.

The routing provider should be selected deliberately based on:

- Accuracy
- Coverage
- Cost
- API limits
- Privacy implications
- Licensing
- Long-term scalability

15.2 Resource Path

A future Resource Path may allow a person to move through multiple resources or service steps.

Example:

Food locker → Shower → ID assistance → Bus stop

This is intentionally excluded from the individual resource map.

It should not influence or complicate the Phase 1 single-resource architecture.

---

16. Multi-Resource Comparison Map

The individual resource map must remain strictly focused on:

Person → One Resource

Multi-resource comparison belongs in a separate, dedicated map.

This is a future feature and must not be mixed into the individual resource card.

16.1 Rationale

Consideration| Multi-Choice in Individual Map| Dedicated Second Map
User focus| Confusing; multiple destinations undermine the simple "get to this place" purpose| Clear; individual card remains focused
UI constraints| Small card becomes crowded with pins and controls| Larger map provides room for comparison
Touch interaction| Multiple controls compete for limited space| Larger interaction targets
API load| Potentially multiplied across every resource card| Triggered only when comparison is requested
Cognitive load| High| Lower

16.2 Recommended future flow

Primary state: Individual Resource Map

The card remains simple.

Point A: Person → Point B: Resource

It provides:

- Resource location
- Person location when available
- Single journey slider
- Estimated straight-line distance
- Relevant contextual information

Secondary state: Comparison Map

Triggered by an action such as:

Compare with nearby options

or a resource-selection checklist.

The comparison view opens a larger map showing:

- The person's location
- Multiple selected resource locations
- Potentially multiple routes in a future routing implementation

The user can then compare or select destinations without complicating the individual resource cards.

Architectural rule

Do not put multi-choice logic into the individual resource map.

The individual map represents one decision.

The comparison map represents multiple decisions.

---

17. Rejected Approaches

These approaches have been tested or evaluated and are not part of the Phase 1 design.

Dashboard scrollbar secretly controlling the map

Rejected because the control's appearance and behavior did not correspond.

A scrollbar should scroll information.

It should not secretly relocate a map indicator.

Full-page GSAP ScrollTrigger pinning

Rejected because the widget is intended to repeat many times on a page.

A single page-level scroll interaction does not scale naturally to hundreds of independent resource widgets.

Freeform map dragging of the journey marker

Rejected because:

- The user's finger can obscure the marker.
- It conflicts with native map panning.
- It introduces unnecessary 2D interaction.

The horizontal journey slider replaces it.

Fake shared starting point

Rejected outright.

Every visitor must not appear to originate from a shared downtown or other arbitrary location.

A false starting point produces false distance information.

